Mitcad patch (mitcad#441): a contact that ends at the vertex where its
blend comes to nothing, on another edge of its face, ends on the spine's
own edge there too.

The rounding round a thread's chamfer cone (0860, one contour) runs along
the cone's edge with an earlier rounding of the crest into the vertex V
where the contour goes on along the cone's edge with the thread's flank;
the flank goes on tangentially from the rounding across an edge from V.
The cone and the rounding meet at 1.7 degrees at V, so the blend is thinner
there than V's tolerance: the walk's contact on the rounding ended at V on
the edge with the flank (ChFi3d_FilCommonPoint takes the vertex within its
tolerance), not on the spine's edge with the cone. The data structure then
held no point of the blend on that spine edge, and the rounding's face kept
the whole edge beside the blend's end, though the blend takes it away: the
face's wire was open and the result invalid. (Patch 0802 handles the same
contact where it ends on the spine's own edge near V.)
ChFi3d_OwnEdgesAtVertices, after ChFi3d_FilDS, adds for such a contact (on
face F, at a vertex V of the edge E between F and the blend's other face
Fo, on an edge of F across which a face Fn goes on tangentially from F, Fn
meeting Fo along an edge from V) a vertex interference on E at V, with the
contact's transition carried over to E as E lies in F.

